 \revminor{The original predictors were restricted to the local 25-cell domain and therefore did not explicitly incorporate atmospheric conditions farther upstream to the west. The C3 gains reported in Section~\ref{sec:predictor_complementarity} were consistent across all five seeds, with both seed-t and moving-block intervals excluding zero (Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_upstream_xgboost} and~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_upstream_bilstm_attn}). Because C3 added variables and spatial coverage together, it does not isolate the effect of extending the domain alone. In the exploratory comparisons, the western near-upstream box (U1) accounted for most of the gain for XGBoost, whereas adding the Atlantic box (U2) to U1 further reduced BiLSTM-Attention RMSE (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3d_upstream_increments} and Fig.~\ref{supp-sfig:r3d_upstream_effects}). In subseasonal downscaling of weekly mean fields, \citet{goutham2023statistical} found that restricting the predictor domain to the predictand domain considerably degraded prediction quality, whereas shifting a predictand-sized domain westward did not improve on a centred one; this provides context rather than validation of six-hour precipitation prediction or of the present boxes. The upstream boxes were fixed before the refits and were not optimized, and only two models, the main chronological split and ERA5 reanalysis inputs were tested; no optimal predictor domain is therefore identified.}
 
 The 6-hour subsampling of the hourly ERA5 data introduces a related constraint. Rapidly developing precipitation systems can evolve on much shorter time scales, particularly during convective events. A six-step input sequence provides 30~h of historical information but cannot resolve sub-hourly or hourly changes in local rainfall structure. Future work could examine whether higher-frequency ERA5 data or complementary radar and satellite observations improve the representation of short-duration events.
+
+\revminor{Reduced-period experiments examined both changes that hourly ERA5 data would allow. Hourly box means for 2017--2021 were used to refit XGBoost and BiLSTM-Attention with five seeds (training 2017--2019, validation 2020, test 2021; Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3hourly_ab} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3hourly_runs}). Configurations A and B use the six six-hourly snapshots of the main configuration or all 31 hourly values over the same 30-h history, with training issue times at 00/06/12/18~UTC (4,374 samples); CA and CB use the same inputs with hourly training issue times (26,244 samples). All four share the validation and test targets, the final-hour target and the 6-h lead time. Relative to A, CB, which combines hourly inputs and hourly issue times, changed overall test RMSE by $-1.57\%$ for XGBoost and $-1.86\%$ for BiLSTM-Attention. Neither change was robust: for XGBoost all five seeds improved and the seed-t interval excluded zero, but the moving-block interval included zero, and for BiLSTM-Attention both intervals included zero. In the exploratory decomposition, hourly inputs alone (B versus A) changed RMSE by $-1.08\%$ and $-0.74\%$, hourly issue times alone (CA versus A) by $-0.92\%$ and $-1.89\%$, and hourly inputs added to hourly issue times (CB versus CA) by $-0.65\%$ and $+0.03\%$. Wet and upper-tail RMSE were 2.3--6.0\% lower for CB than for A in all five seeds of both models, with both intervals excluding zero; these subset results are exploratory and unadjusted for multiplicity. Experiment C was designed after the input-frequency results had been seen, the evaluation covers one test year and two models, and the hourly training samples are highly autocorrelated (lag-1 autocorrelation 0.89), so the effective sample size increases far less than sixfold; with hourly issue times, each training epoch also contains about six times as many optimization steps. A robust overall benefit is therefore not established, and its absence is not established either, for the main 2004--2021 configuration.}
 
 {\color{red}
 A further limitation is the relative scarcity of high-intensity regional-mean precipitation: only 402 training samples reached 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$. The loss and weighting sensitivity experiments have already examined Huber, pseudo-Huber, sample weighting, log1p-target and Tweedie objectives in the specified model settings (Section~\ref{sec:training_objective_sensitivity}). Huber worsened BiLSTM-Attention but improved the Transformer in overall RMSE at $\delta=2$. Both Transformer configurations frequently approached the 30-epoch limit, and its wet- and upper-tail seed-based intervals included zero; no intrinsic advantage under fully converged training is established. XGBoost weighting improved tail RMSE on five-seed mean predictions at a substantial cost in overall error and M3's own positive bias. This aggregation is distinct from the mean of five seed-level RMSE values in the main comparison. These experiments do not exhaust objective functions or model configurations and cannot identify a dominant cause of underestimation. The peak-timing diagnostic is limited to neighborhoods of the largest sampled amounts, with overlapping windows and a selected sample that is not a local observed maximum; it does not establish delays of independent storm peaks.
